  "narrative": "The differential metabolite signature is dominated by arachidonic acid (C00219)–derived eicosanoids and sulfur amino acid intermediates, pointing to two interconnected metabolic perturbations.\n\n**Eicosanoid synthesis** (WikiPathways WP167) is the top-ranked pathway (FDR = 0.0, fold-enrichment = 1,251), with five matched input compounds: arachidonic acid, leukotriene A4, 15-deoxy-Δ¹²,¹⁴-PGJ₂, 5-HETE, and 15-HETE. Arachidonic acid sits at the apex of this cascade, and the KEGG reaction graph confirms it is a direct precursor to leukotriene A4 (via the 5-lipoxygenase product 5-HPETE; path C00219 → C05356 → C00909) and to 5-HETE (same 2-hop route in the forward direction). This establishes arachidonic acid as the primary driver of the leukotriene and HETE arms of the pathway.\n\n**Selenium micronutrient network** (WikiPathways WP15) is the second candidate (FDR = 0.0, fold-enrichment = 365), with six matched compounds including L-cysteine (C00097) and L-cystathionine (C02291). Membership queries confirm both are annotated in this pathway (L-cystathionine explicitly in cysteine/methionine catabolism, L-cysteine in multiple sulfur-handling pathways), and both co-occur with the eicosanoid compounds (co-occurrence plausibility = 1.0). L-cysteine is a known precursor for glutathione synthesis and for the trans-sulfuration pathway that generates H₂S, linking the sulfur amino acid and eicosanoid axes.\n\nThe remaining compound, mercaptopurine (C02380), is a structural analog of hypoxanthine and a known antimetabolite used in immunosuppression and leukemia therapy; its appearance here suggests disruption of purine salvage, though it does not enrich for arachidonic acid pathways.\n\nIn summary, the treatment group exhibits coordinated elevation of arachidonic acid–derived lipid mediators and sulfur amino acid intermediates, implicating bidirectional activation of the 5-lipoxygenase/leukotriene axis and the trans-sulfuration pathway. The verified upstream position of arachidonic acid makes it the most plausible driver of the eicosanoid arm, while L-cysteine/L-cystathionine alterations may reflect altered redox or inflammatory tone.",
